NNAAMMEE
     wwhheerreeiiss — locate programs

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     wwhheerreeiiss [--aabbmmqqssuuxx] [--BBMMSS _d_i_r _._._. --ff] _p_r_o_g_r_a_m _._._.

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The wwhheerreeiiss utility checks the standard binary, manual page, and source
     directories for the specified programs, printing out the paths of any it
     finds.  The supplied program names are first stripped of leading path
     name components, any single trailing extension added by gzip(1),
     compress(1), or bzip2(1), and the leading ‘s.’ or trailing ‘,v’ from a
     source code control system.

     The default path searched is the string returned by the sysctl(8) utility
     for the “user.cs_path” string, with _/_u_s_r_/_l_i_b_e_x_e_c and the current user's
     $PATH appended.  Manual pages are searched by default along the $MANPATH.
     Program sources are located in a list of known standard places, including
     all the subdirectories of _/_u_s_r_/_s_r_c and _/_u_s_r_/_p_o_r_t_s.

     The following options are available:

     --BB      Specify directories to search for binaries.  Requires the --ff op‐
             tion.

     --MM      Specify directories to search for manual pages.  Requires the --ff
             option.

     --SS      Specify directories to search for program sources.  Requires the
             --ff option.

     --aa      Report all matches instead of only the first of each requested
             type.

     --bb      Search for binaries.

     --ff      Delimits the list of directories after the --BB, --MM, or --SS options,
             and indicates the beginning of the _p_r_o_g_r_a_m list.

     --mm      Search for manual pages.

     --qq      (“quiet”).  Suppress the output of the utility name in front of
             the normal output line.  This can become handy for use in a back‐
             quote substitution of a shell command line, see _E_X_A_M_P_L_E_S.

     --ss      Search for source directories.

     --uu      Search for “unusual” entries.  A file is said to be unusual if it
             does not have at least one entry of each requested type.  Only
             the name of the unusual entry is printed.

     --xx      Do not use “expensive” tools when searching for source directo‐
             ries.  Normally, after unsuccessfully searching all the first-
             level subdirectories of the source directory list, wwhheerreeiiss will
             ask locate(1) to find the entry on its behalf.  Since this can
             take much longer, it can be turned off with --xx.

EEXXAAMMPPLLEESS
     The following finds all utilities under _/_u_s_r_/_b_i_n that do not have docu‐
     mentation:

           whereis -m -u /usr/bin/*

     Change to the source code directory of ls(1):

           cd `whereis -sq ls`

SSEEEE AALLSSOO
     find(1), locate(1), man(1), which(1), sysctl(8)

HHIISSTTOORRYY
     The wwhheerreeiiss utility appeared in 3.0BSD.  This version re-implements the
     historical functionality that was lost in 4.4BSD.

AAUUTTHHOORRSS
     This implementation of the wwhheerreeiiss command was written by Jörg Wunsch.

BBUUGGSS
     This re-implementation of the wwhheerreeiiss utility is not bug-for-bug compati‐
     ble with historical versions.  It is believed to be compatible with the
     version that was shipping with FreeBSD 2.2 through FreeBSD 4.5 though.

     The wwhheerreeiiss utility can report some unrelated source entries when the --aa
     option is specified.

